                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the control system that governs the plan. A <strong>finance-only scorecard</strong> (revenue, EBIT, ROCE) detects the miss too late; a <strong>forty-metric dashboard</strong> drowns the signal. You want a <strong>balanced</strong> set: lagging financials plus the two or three <em>leading indicators</em> that flag a slip six months early — contract-pipeline conversion, platform-qualification milestones, time-to-ramp. Then decide how to use them with Reuter: <strong>diagnostically</strong> on execution and <strong>interactively</strong> to surface the unsigned-contract risk together (Simons' levers).`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set the budget targets`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Now set the targets the board approves. Anchor on the president's <strong>heroic top-down</strong> and you guarantee variance and gaming; anchor on the <strong>sandbagged bottom-up</strong> and you bake in the padding and waste the division's potential. <strong>Counter-flow</strong> negotiates the two against each other. Decide the contingency <strong>buffer</strong> against the unsigned-contract risk (62% of the growth is speculative), handle the sandbagging without a witch-hunt, and put an <em>honest</em> target-achievement probability to the board — not the optimistic 90%+.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Challenge the strategy and write the opinion`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the CFO and President Reuter <strong>together</strong>. Calibrate your stance: <strong>scorekeeper</strong> records and caveats, <strong>business partner</strong> reshapes and keeps the seat, <strong>adversary</strong> guts the plan in public and gets controlling cut out. Recast the €180M as a <strong>milestone-gated</strong> release — tranches tied to committed volume, Contract A signing, and qualification — and deliver an opinion the board can act on: <em>approve, approve-with-gates, or reshape</em>. "Here are the risks" is not a decision.`,

                    r1_bridge_label:        `Decompose the revenue bridge: how do you classify the €660M of promised growth?`,
                    bridge_separate:        `Separate it — a defensible committed base, with the unsigned-contract growth flagged as speculative upside`,
                    bridge_probable:        `Treat the two unsigned contracts as probable — they will likely close, so book most of the growth`,
                    bridge_endorse:         `Endorse the full €1.3bn — the president knows his market better than controlling does`,
                    r1_benchmark_label:     `Benchmark the assumptions against the division's ±22% forecast record. What's your read?`,
                    bench_outsideview:      `Take the outside view — a ±22% record signals optimism bias; model a realistic-volume scenario`,
                    bench_trend:            `Trust the trend — electrification is different, so the historical error doesn't apply this time`,
                    bench_ignore:           `Ignore it — forecast accuracy is a sales problem, not a controlling concern`,
                    r1_sandbag_label:       `How candidly do you flag the two sandbagged cost centres and the over-stretched top-line? (%)`,
                    r1_candor_readout:      `Willingness to name padding and stretch by evidence, not blame:`,

                    r2_balance_label:       `What KPI architecture governs the plan?`,
                    bal_balanced:           `Balanced — lagging financials (revenue, EBIT, ROCE) plus leading indicators tied to the value drivers`,
                    bal_financeonly:        `Finance-only — revenue, EBIT margin and ROCE; the numbers that matter to the board`,
                    bal_vanity:             `A wide dashboard — forty metrics so nothing important is ever missed`,
                    r2_leading_label:       `Pick the 2–3 leading indicators that would flag a miss earliest`,
                    ld_pipeline:            `Contract-pipeline conversion on the two EV platforms`,
                    ld_qual:                `Platform-qualification milestones passed`,
                    ld_ramp:                `Time-to-ramp and scrap/quality on the new lines`,
                    ld_revenue:             `Monthly revenue actuals vs plan`,
                    ld_share:               `Reported share price and analyst sentiment`,
                    r2_leading_hint:        `Leading indicators predict the miss before the lagging numbers move. Pick the few that give the earliest, actionable warning — not lagging results dressed up as early signals.`,
                    r2_use_label:           `How will the chosen KPIs be used with the president (Simons' levers)?`,
                    use_both:               `Both — diagnostic control on execution AND interactive use to surface the unsigned-contract risk with him`,
                    use_diagnostic:         `Diagnostic only — set targets and report variances and exceptions monthly`,
                    use_report:             `Just report the numbers up the line; the president runs his own strategy`,

                    r3_philosophy_label:    `Which target-setting philosophy anchors the budget?`,
                    phil_counterflow:       `Counter-flow — negotiate top-down ambition against truthful bottom-up, stripping padding and stretch`,
                    phil_topdown:           `Top-down stretch — anchor on the president's heroic €1.3bn so the division reaches`,
                    phil_bottomup:          `Bottom-up — anchor on the cost-centre budgets; it looks prudent and is easy to defend`,
                    r3_buffer_label:        `Contingency buffer against the unsigned-contract risk: what share of the upside do you hold back? (%)`,
                    r3_buffer_hint:         `Hold back too little and a missed contract blows the target; hold back too much and you waste the division's real potential. The unsigned contracts are 62% of the growth.`,
                    r3_sandbag_label:       `How do you handle the two sandbagged cost centres?`,
                    sand_evidence:          `Re-anchor their numbers on evidence and benchmarks, openly, without a witch-hunt`,
                    sand_bake:              `Leave the padded numbers in — it makes the division's targets safe and easy to hit`,
                    sand_hunt:              `Expose and publicly punish the two managers to deter padding`,
                    r3_achiev_label:        `Target-achievement probability you'll present to the board for the realistic scenario (%)`,
                    r3_achiev_readout:      `Honest probability of hitting the proposed targets:`,

                    r4_stance_label:        `Where do you stand on the scorekeeper–partner–watchdog spectrum?`,
                    stance_partner:         `Business partner — constructively reshape the plan and keep a seat at the strategy table`,
                    stance_scorekeeper:     `Scorekeeper — record the plan, note the caveats, let the board decide`,
                    stance_adversary:       `Adversary — publicly contest the plan and call it unrealistic in front of the board`,
                    r4_gate_label:          `How do you release the €180M capital request?`,
                    gate_gated:             `Milestone-gated — Tranche 1 on committed volume, Tranche 2 when Contract A signs, Tranche 3 on qualification`,
                    gate_upfront:           `All €180M up front — the plan needs the capacity in place to win the contracts`,
                    gate_freeze:            `Freeze all capital until both contracts are signed and volumes proven`,
                    r4_opinion_label:       `What controlling opinion do you put to the board?`,
                    op_gates:               `Approve-with-gates — back the ambition, recast the heroic targets as conditional upside, gate the capital`,
                    op_approve:             `Approve — endorse the plan and the €1.3bn as presented`,
                    op_reshape:             `Reshape — send the plan back; the targets and capital case are not yet defensible`,
                    op_risks:               `No recommendation — present the full risk analysis and let the board decide`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend the opinion with the president sitting next to you? (%)`,

                    warn_candor_range:      `Your candor level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_leading_count:     `Pick 2 or 3 leading indicators — a focused set, not all five and not just one.`,
                    warn_buffer_range:      `The contingency buffer must be a percentage between 0 and 60.`,
                    warn_achiev_range:      `The achievement probability must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_separate:      `<strong>That's the controller's read.</strong> You separated a defensible committed base from the unsigned-contract growth and flagged the latter as speculative upside — not a single heroic number. Now the board can see what it's really betting €180M on.`,
                    coach_r1_probable:      `<strong>"Probable" is doing a lot of work there.</strong> You've booked most of the growth on two contracts that aren't signed, in a division that forecasts within ±22%. What's your number if neither lands — and why isn't that scenario in your opinion?`,
                    coach_r1_endorse:       `<strong>You've endorsed €1.3bn untested.</strong> Sixty-two percent of that rides on unsigned contracts. The last division that hit every slide missed reality by 30%. Controlling that rubber-stamps optimism owns the credibility hit when it misses.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You decomposed the bridge AND took the outside view on a ±22% record — that's debiasing top-management optimism with evidence, exactly what a controller is for. The committed base is defensible; the rest is honest upside.`,
                    coach_r1_trend:         `<strong>"This time is different" is the optimist's anthem.</strong> A persistent ±22% error is a systematic bias signal, not noise that electrification cancels. Model a realistic-volume scenario or you're endorsing the over-confidence you're meant to check.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Forecast accuracy is squarely a controlling concern.</strong> A division that misses by ±22% and books €1.3bn is exactly where the management control system earns its keep. Measuring and correcting the bias is your job, not sales' problem.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>You're pulling your punches.</strong> Naming the sandbagged centres and the stretch by evidence — not blame — is how a controller stays both honest and credible. Stay this quiet and the board never sees the padding.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced architecture is the right spine.</strong> Lagging financials tell the board the result; leading indicators tell you in time to steer. That's a control system that enables decisions rather than just confirming them after the year is gone.`,
                    coach_r2_financeonly:   `<strong>Revenue and EBIT are both lagging.</strong> By the time revenue misses, the year is gone and so is the capital. What would have warned you six months early — qualification milestones, pipeline conversion, scrap on the new line?`,
                    coach_r2_vanity:        `<strong>Forty metrics is a dashboard nobody acts on.</strong> A control system that measures everything steers nothing. Pick the few leading indicators tied to the real value drivers, with owners, cadence and triggers.`,
                    coach_r2_lagging:       `<strong>Monthly revenue actuals are lagging, not leading.</strong> They confirm the miss after it happens. A leading indicator moves <em>before</em> the revenue line does — that's the whole point of early warning.`,
                    coach_r2_noise:         `<strong>Share price and analyst sentiment aren't your control levers.</strong> They're noisy, externally driven, and tell you nothing actionable about whether the ramp is slipping. Don't dress market noise up as a KPI.`,
                    coach_r2_leadingsharp:  `<strong>That's the early-warning trio.</strong> Pipeline conversion, qualification milestones and time-to-ramp would each flag a slip months before revenue moves — leading indicators tied to the plan's true value drivers.`,
                    coach_r2_both:          `<strong>Diagnostic plus interactive is the Simons play.</strong> Govern execution with diagnostic KPIs, and use a few interactively to surface the unsigned-contract risk <em>with</em> Reuter — that keeps you in the strategy conversation, not just reporting on it.`,
                    coach_r2_diagnostic:    `<strong>Diagnostic-only control is half the toolkit.</strong> Variance reports govern execution, but they don't engage the president on the emerging strategic risk. Use a few KPIs interactively or you'll surface the contract risk too late.`,
                    coach_r2_report:        `<strong>Reporting numbers up the line cedes the strategy seat.</strong> If the president runs his own strategy and you just report, the control system never debiases the plan — and you've made controlling the back office the CFO feared.`,
                    coach_r3_counterflow:   `<strong>Counter-flow is the honest middle.</strong> You negotiated top-down ambition against truthful bottom-up, stripping both padding and stretch. That extracts real information and motivates without inviting sandbagging or gaming.`,
                    coach_r3_topdown:       `<strong>Anchoring on the heroic €1.3bn guarantees variance.</strong> Top-down stretch targets invite gaming and demotivation, and lock in the very optimism the CFO asked you to check. A target you can't honestly defend isn't a target.`,
                    coach_r3_bottomup:      `<strong>Bottom-up looks prudent and bakes in the padding.</strong> Two cost centres sandbagged to protect their bonuses — anchor on their numbers and you'll hit the target easily and waste the division's real potential. Prudent on the surface, slack underneath.`,
                    coach_r3_thinbuffer:    `<strong>That buffer is too thin.</strong> 62% of the growth depends on contracts that aren't signed. Hold back almost nothing and a single missed contract blows the whole target — and your credibility with it.`,
                    coach_r3_fatbuffer:     `<strong>That buffer is too fat.</strong> Hold back most of the upside and you've quietly re-created sandbagging at the divisional level — waste the real potential and the president rightly objects. Calibrate the holdback to the 62% at risk.`,
                    coach_r3_bake:          `<strong>Leaving the padding in is baking sandbagging into the targets.</strong> Safe and easy to hit, yes — and exactly the slack the CFO wants controlling to strip out. You'll reward the padding and miss the division's real number.`,
                    coach_r3_hunt:          `<strong>A public witch-hunt poisons the well.</strong> You may deter padding once, but you've taught every manager to hide information from controlling. Re-anchor on evidence openly — fix the numbers without making enemies of the people who feed you data.`,
                    coach_r3_overconfident: `<strong>A 90%+ probability is the rubber stamp in disguise.</strong> With ±22% error and 62% unsigned, an honest realistic-scenario hit rate is well short of certain. Present a probability you can defend, not the optimism the board already fell for.`,
                    coach_r3_underconfident:`<strong>An ultra-low probability undersells a defensible plan.</strong> If you've stripped the padding and gated the risk, the realistic scenario is achievable more often than that. Don't swing from rubber stamp to doom-monger — calibrate.`,
                    coach_r4_partner:       `<strong>Business partner is the calibrated stance here.</strong> You can reshape the plan and keep your seat at the strategy table — challenge the numbers without becoming the business-prevention department. That's control as enabling, not just policing.`,
                    coach_r4_scorekeeper:   `<strong>Pure scorekeeping lets a fragile plan pass.</strong> Recording the plan and noting caveats is safe and weak — the board asked for a judgement, not minutes. You're leaving the debiasing job half-done.`,
                    coach_r4_adversary:     `<strong>You were right on the numbers and lost the room.</strong> Reuter has told the CFO that controlling doesn't understand the EV business and should stick to bookkeeping. Gut the plan in public and you're cut out of the strategy process entirely — the worst outcome for a controller meant to enable better decisions.`,
                    coach_r4_partnergate:   `<strong>Partner plus gates is the textbook resolution.</strong> You backed the ambition, kept the relationship, and converted an all-or-nothing €180M bet into a staged, evidence-driven commitment. That makes the plan more likely to succeed, not less.`,
                    coach_r4_upfront:       `<strong>All €180M up front, on volumes that assume both contracts.</strong> If the realistic case lands, the return is marginal and you've sunk the capital. Why not gate it — Tranche 1 on committed volume, the rest as the contracts de-risk?`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing all capital is the watchdog overreaching.</strong> Wait for both signatures and proven volumes and the division can't build the capacity it needs to win the contracts at all. You've protected the balance sheet by strangling the strategy — and lost the president.`,
                    coach_r4_approve:       `<strong>Approving the plan as presented is the rubber stamp.</strong> You've endorsed the heroic €1.3bn and the full capital request untested. When it misses by the division's usual ±22%, controlling owns the credibility hit the CFO warned you about.`,
                    coach_r4_reshape:       `<strong>Reshape is defensible but costs you the ambition.</strong> Sending the whole plan back is honest on the numbers, but it reads as obstruction to a board that loved the story — and risks the president freezing controlling out. A gated approval challenges and preserves the strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_norisk:        `<strong>"Here are the risks" is not a decision.</strong> The board asked for an opinion — approve, approve-with-gates, or reshape — by the date. A thorough analysis with no recommendation leaves the judgement undone. What do you actually recommend, with Reuter sitting next to you?`,
                    coach_r4_lowconviction: `<strong>You hedged in front of the president.</strong> If you can't defend the opinion with Reuter in the room, the board can't act on it and the CFO can't trust it. Calibrated conviction — firm on the judgement, open on the assumptions — is what earns the seat.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Separated committed from heroic; took the outside view candidly.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Under-tested the bridge or pulled punches on the optimism.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Balanced lead+lag KPIs, used diagnostically and interactively.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Lagging-only or unfocused KPIs, or ceded the strategy seat.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Counter-flow targets, calibrated buffer, sandbag re-anchored on evidence.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Anchored on stretch or padding, or mis-sized the buffer.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Business partner, milestone-gated capital, approve-with-gates.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Rubber-stamped, lost the seat, or gave no actionable opinion.`,

                    insight_trusted:        `Trusted controlling opinion: you debiased the optimism with evidence, built a balanced control system with real early-warning indicators, set counter-flow targets, gated the capital, and stayed a business partner. The board gets a judgement it can act on and controlling keeps its seat at the table.`,
                    insight_credible:       `Credible check: you challenged the plan on evidence and avoided rubber-stamping it. To reach a trusted opinion, tighten the leading indicators, calibrate the buffer to the 62% at risk, and convert the €180M into a staged, gated release.`,
                    insight_weak:           `The opinion didn't land. Either the plan passed under-tested, the KPIs were too lagging to steer, the targets were anchored on stretch or padding, or the stance cost you the seat. Re-trace the five classic errors against your choices.`,
                    insight_rubberstamp:    `Rubber-stamp signal: somewhere you endorsed the optimistic case untested — the full bridge, a 90%+ hit rate, all the capital up front, or an unconditional approval. That's exactly the credibility trap the CFO had been burned by.`,
                    insight_lostseat:       `You lost the seat at the strategy table. An over-adversarial stance can be right on the numbers and still get controlling cut out of the strategy process — the worst outcome for a function meant to enable better decisions, not just police them.`,
                    insight_nodecision:     `Analysis without a decision: a thorough risk picture is not an opinion the board can act on. Controlling has to convert the analysis into an accountable approve / approve-with-gates / reshape judgement by the date.`,
                    insight_gated:          `Milestone-gated capital: by releasing in tranches tied to committed volume, Contract A signing and qualification, you converted an all-or-nothing bet on optimistic volumes into a staged, evidence-driven real option. Far less exposed if the optimism is wrong.`,
                    insight_exposed:        `Capital exposure stayed high: releasing most of the €180M before the contracts de-risk leaves the division — and your opinion — heavily exposed to the very volumes the ±22% record says may not arrive.`,
                    insight_starved:        `You froze nearly all the capital. Safe for the balance sheet, but the division can't build the capacity it needs to win the contracts at all — that's the watchdog overreaching and starving a strategy you were meant to enable, not just police.`,

                    hint_lostseat:          `You're being read as the business-prevention department. Challenge the plan as a business partner — reshape and gate it — instead of contesting it in public.`,
                    hint_rubberstamp:       `You're endorsing the optimistic case untested. Take the outside view, gate the capital, and present an honest achievement probability — not the 90% the board already loved.`,
                    hint_influence:         `Your seat at the strategy table is slipping. A controller cut out of strategy can't debias anything — recover influence by reshaping with the president, not against him.`,
                    hint_cred:              `Plan credibility is low with the board. Separate committed from speculative growth, calibrate the buffer to the unsigned-contract risk, and gate the capital.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — control quality, credibility and influence all healthy. Lock it in with a milestone-gated capital release and an approve-with-gates opinion you can defend with Reuter in the room.`,

